Answer: A statement is one complete instruction in a program, such as printing something or declaring a variable. Execution always starts from the main method, and the statements run one below the other, in the same order in which they are written. This is why Eclipse shows line numbers: the control moves line by line. In the program below, the main method has one statement that prints StringStack.

```java
public class Demo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
System.out.println("StringStack");
}
}
```

Output: StringStack

Answer: When you create a variable inside a class, it belongs to that class. For example, the variable marks of type int is created inside the Demo class. A method created inside the class also belongs to that class, and it is called by its name from the main method. In the same way, the Java developers created the variable out inside the System class, and the println method inside the PrintStream class. That is why System.out.println() works the same way as the variables and methods you write yourself.

```java
public class Demo {
int marks = 90;                       // variable belongs to the Demo class
static void myMethod() {              // method belongs to the Demo class
System.out.println("method");
}
public static void main(String[] args) {
myMethod();
}
}
```

Output: method

Answer: The simple solution is to copy and paste the statement five times. It works, but it has a limitation: if the same task has to be repeated 100 or 1000 times, you would need that many copies of the code. Duplicating the same code like this is called code redundancy. Redundant code is hard to maintain and should be avoided; a task that repeats should never be written again and again.

```java
public class Demo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
System.out.println("StringStack");
System.out.println("StringStack");
System.out.println("StringStack");
System.out.println("StringStack");
System.out.println("StringStack");
}
}
```

Output: StringStack StringStack StringStack StringStack StringStack
